for those of you listening guys i knew  steve in college  and um his nickname was  mr mayor which i think you even had in  high school a little bit  um and i don't know how to explain it  guys like so we went we went to duke  university in in durham north carolina  and  it's a it's not it's six thousand  undergrad is like ten thousand  with with grad school kids it's  relatively big um but everybody knew  glonis  and it was because of these random he  was always doing a different venture and  the big one was  uh throwing parties in college there's  spartan entertainment and he was known  for  wednesday night shooters night which  shooters is a famous bar at duke it's  like a western themed bar and it's a  piece of crap  but wednesdays nothing was going on and  stephen's like well look if i can get  people to show up on wednesday and they  pay five bucks at the door can i get a  cut that sort of thing i'm pretty sure  that's how it started so  talk to me about your  entrepreneurial journey because to me i  always like to consider myself a natural  builder but  nowhere near it seems like it was in  your dna probably even younger than  college i imagine so tell us about you  and your background a bit  yeah it's funny thinking back to the  spartan entertainment days but  you know in many ways when people ask me  what you know i majored in at duke like  i say entrepreneurship we didn't have a  major for  business or entrepreneurship there but  that's what i did  you know that that that's what i spend  my time doing and you know for those  that  didn't go to duke between uh 2006 and  2010  um this was a really interesting age  where facebook was brand new  um you know zac and i were the first  grade  of college students that actually had  facebook for throughout the entire  day of our college experience and back  then you had to have the  edu address to even uh get onto the  network so i remember  uh you know when i was a senior in high  school people would like  apply early to a safety school and put  the deposit down  just to get the edu address so they  could join the facebook at the time  it was that pretentious and exclusive it  was a big freaking deal  and um one of the things that you know  we  noticed that was really different from  for people like zach and i  versus any other generation that had  ever gone to college was that we had  this thing facebook  and we were connected with every single  person that we met in  in many ways like we're still connected  right like you know all those people you  friended from your freshman year dorm on  facebook  you know they've been following  everything that you've been doing with  your charity with you know with the yang  campaign  so in many ways like we had this  built-in distribution network  and we kind of came along at the right  time back then  bar owners were not able to be on  facebook so  back in the day when you know you  created a facebook group and people  joined and you created an event and  people showed up  uh me and a guy named zach maris who's  another  uh entrepreneur who was a senior when i  was a freshman  and is now the ceo of a very successful  tech startup back in durham called  teamworks  zach and i had the idea to start  throwing  non-affiliated parties because while  zach and i were friends we were in  different fraternities so  if sigma nu was throwing a party the  delta 6 would show up  if the atos were throwing a party the  saes wouldn't come  but zach and i decided to create our own  branded parties that were unaffiliated  and slowly but surely everybody started  coming so it didn't matter  what fraternity you were in it didn't  matter if you were in a fraternity it  didn't matter if you were  in band or a football player like you  came to our parties  and um and over a four-year period we  took  starting with one event which was uh  putting beer pong tables out  uh on wednesday night at like the big  bar on campus that used to be closed and  offering three dollar pictures  and taking the cover charge um we ended  up having over  17 000 college students in this facebook  group  and started a variety of businesses uh  off this key asset so we had a college  boxes  business for a couple years we had a hot  dog stand at one point  uh we did dj equipment and t-shirt  rentals and  venue bookings like you name it zach and  i were doing it  and i think it really gave us uh our  first  taste of entrepreneurship and for zach  you know that capital from spartan  entertainment was a lot of the seed  money that started his business and he's  continued to do that uh today and like i  said it's a very successful business  and for me uh this you know taste of  entrepreneurship was something that i  could never  fully get out of my mouth and when i  ended up leaving durham  and going to be a trader for a few years  and working at linkedin  like i did other cool things but that  entrepreneurial  bug kept you know kept nagging at me  and i really believe that like the three  percent of people  on earth who have that mutation in their  brain that makes them like a true  entrepreneur uh it's almost impossible  for those people to  to do great like the best work of their  lives if they're working for somewhere  out someone  someone else and i would put myself in  that case  and uh and for me you know cameo has  been something that's really  at that guy point for me ikigai is this  japanese philosophy  that believes that your true life's  calling is the intersection of  what do you love to do what are you  great at what does the world need and  what can you ultimately get paid for  and it has to be at that like pure  intersection point  and you know zach i'll ask you uh you  know you've known me for a long time  like is this bit is is the fact that i  started this business surprising to you  oh dude not not at all um you've got  these  smartest minds in the world in many ways  at duke university  and they're all like  slaving away at their econ exams and  their chemistry exams just  and you're out there throwing parties  which seems like a joke  um but on the other hand you're learning  10 times more like because  like you're negotiating deals and you're  going to contracts and booking venues  and recruiting people and developing a  marketing plan and  like raising capital and like investing  in new businesses i figured i knew like  smart entertainment was like the tip of  the iceberg for you but i was more  surprised when you went i think you were  a traitor and you worked at linkedin and  i was like  i knew that wasn't the end game either  so i was so like um  but i remember getting an email from you  it was a survey monkey survey like hey  we've got this idea  where famous people  can get paid to facetime their fans  what do you think of these names and it  was like this long survey  of a bunch of names uh cameo was one of  them if i recall but there were a bunch  of others  um nothing personal cameo wasn't my top  vote  uh i don't remember what what it was i  probably looked it up but it's fun it's  funny  we were just talking about this survey  and um  cameo wasn't on the original list  actually there were four  there were four and i will tell this is  exclusive nobody else has ever heard  this story  so i hope your listeners enjoy uh we  we hired a branding firm in miami called  vanilla shake who's incredibly talented  and we went through the exhaustive  process of like  naming and in a consumer internet  business like a name is so  critical and when we first started the  business  we we didn't know what the name was so  we were using  um well so there are two things that  happen number one my co-founder  martin uh his family businesses in the  uk are all  barren like barren homes and baron  finance and things like that  so he wanted to call the business baron  app and  they were and and actually today that is  the legal name of the company the legal  name of the company  is baron app inc something tell me when  you guys go public the new york city  stock exchange listing  is gonna bear an app llc  and it's funny because we had actually  never um  we had never nobody's ever like called  us bear an app before but in the wall  street journal  like funding announcement they always  use the legal name of the business  so it was talking about baron app inc  valued at a billion dollars the parent  company of cameo  so it was just kind of interesting and  and but we knew  baron app could not be like what we  called this business  at the time we decided to use a code  name and we called it uh power move  so pow it was called project power move  was was  was basically what we called it in the  branding firm that we hired came up with  three names uh first was hero hub  uh so almost like it was like a hub to  find your heroes  the second name was thrillo and the  third name was hyped  i can tell h-y-p-d bunch of dudes are  making this this company  we put this name out these four names  out  to all of our uh followers on  linkedin we collectively downloaded all  of our linkedin contacts  and put the survey monkey out and  unfortunately power move  won so we knew we didn't have a great  name  and some of the feedback the branding  firm personally had recommended hero hub  and for our east coasters everyone  felt that that sounded like a sandwich  shop which was  you know kind of funny uh so ultimately  we you know went back to the drawing  board and my little brother  who was working for us at the time was  like i think it should be called  cameo he's like these people are kind of  making a cameo  in your life and immediately it was like  thank you little bro  that has to be the name of course  cameo.com was not available  and seemed to be impossible to get  uh all the social handles weren't  available  so what we decided to do is we bought  book cameo  verb in front of it it made all of the  socials book  cameo for a while but for the first  couple years of the business  maybe people just thought it was called  book cameo so that's  so that's a a little you know fun story  about  how cameo got its name  [Music]  you

